# Important Battles of India

> Battle questions are the most formulaic in the history section: a year, two
> names, and a winner. SSC, RRB and police papers rarely go beyond a list of
> about twenty famous engagements. Learn them in four eras (ancient and
> medieval, Mughal, colonial, and independent India) and almost every battle
> question becomes a quick match.

## 1. Ancient and Early Medieval Battles

| Battle | Year | Fought between | Result |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| **Hydaspes** (Jhelum) | 326 BCE | Alexander and **Porus** | Alexander won, but restored Porus to his kingdom |
| **Kalinga War** | 261 BCE | **Ashoka** and Kalinga | Ashoka won; remorse led him toward Buddhism |
| **First Battle of Tarain** | 1191 | **Prithviraj Chauhan** and Muhammad of Ghor | Prithviraj won |
| **Second Battle of Tarain** | 1192 | Prithviraj Chauhan and Muhammad of Ghor | Muhammad of Ghor won; Turkish rule rooted in north India |
| **Chandawar** | 1194 | Muhammad of Ghor and **Jaichand** of Kannauj | Muhammad of Ghor won |

- Tarain is near Karnal in present Haryana, a regular Haryana-exam fact.
- The **Battle of Raichur** (1520) saw **Krishnadevaraya** of Vijayanagara defeat
  Ismail Adil Shah of Bijapur.
- The **Battle of Talikota** (also called Rakshasa-Tangadi, 1565) broke
  Vijayanagara: the Deccan sultanates defeated it and **Rama Raya** was killed.

## 2. Battles of the Mughal Age

| Battle | Year | Fought between | Result |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| **First Battle of Panipat** | 1526 | **Babur** and **Ibrahim Lodi** | Babur won; Mughal rule began |
| **Khanwa** | 1527 | Babur and **Rana Sanga** of Mewar | Babur won |
| **Chanderi** | 1528 | Babur and **Medini Rai** | Babur won |
| **Ghaghra** | 1529 | Babur and the Afghans of the east | Babur won |
| **Chausa** | 1539 | **Sher Shah Suri** and **Humayun** | Sher Shah won |
| **Kannauj (Bilgram)** | 1540 | Sher Shah and Humayun | Sher Shah won; Humayun fled India |
| **Second Battle of Panipat** | 1556 | **Akbar** (under Bairam Khan) and **Hemu** | Mughals won |
| **Haldighati** | 1576 | Akbar's army under **Man Singh** and **Maharana Pratap** | Mughal army prevailed on the field; Pratap escaped |
| **Samugarh** | 1658 | **Aurangzeb** and **Dara Shikoh** | Aurangzeb won |
| **Saraighat** | 1671 | Ahoms under **Lachit Borphukan** and Mughals under Ram Singh | Ahoms won |
| **Third Battle of Panipat** | 1761 | **Ahmad Shah Abdali** and the Marathas under Sadashivrao Bhau | Abdali won |

[definition]
A **decisive battle** is one whose outcome changes who rules a region. In Indian
exams the three at Panipat (1526, 1556, 1761) are the standard examples: each
changed the balance of power in north India.
[/definition]

[tip]
Memory trick for Panipat: 1526 Babur, 1556 Akbar, 1761 Marathas and Abdali.
Years climb by 30 and then by 205, and the first two winners are Mughals.
[/tip]

- **Hemu**, a Hindu general of the Suri rulers, briefly held Delhi in 1556.
- **Khanwa** broke the Rajput confederacy led by Rana Sanga.
- **Lachit Borphukan** is honoured on Lachit Diwas in Assam.

## 3. Battles of the Colonial Period

| Battle or war | Year | Fought between | Result |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| **Plassey** | 23 June 1757 | **Robert Clive** and **Siraj-ud-Daulah** | British won, helped by Mir Jafar's betrayal |
| **Wandiwash** | 1760 | **Eyre Coote** and Lally (French) | British won; end of French power in India (Third Carnatic War) |
| **Buxar** | 22 October 1764 | **Hector Munro** against Mir Qasim, Shuja-ud-Daula and Shah Alam II | British won; Treaty of Allahabad 1765 |
| **Anglo-Mysore Wars** | 1767 to 1799 | Mysore (Hyder Ali, Tipu Sultan) and the British | Fourth war (1799): Tipu died at Seringapatam |
| **Anglo-Maratha Wars** | 1775 to 1819 | Marathas and the British | Peshwa rule ended after the third war |
| **Anglo-Sikh Wars** | 1845 to 1849 | Sikh Empire and the British | Punjab annexed in 1849 |
| **Koregaon** | 1 January 1818 | Peshwa's forces and a British force | Part of the Third Anglo-Maratha War |

- Plassey is a battle in name; its true significance was political, since
  the British gained influence in Bengal. **Buxar** was the real battle that
  gave the British legal rights, when the Mughal emperor granted the **diwani**
  of Bengal, Bihar and Orissa in 1765.
- The four Anglo-Mysore Wars: **1767 to 69**, **1780 to 84**, **1790 to 92**
  and **1799**. The treaties are Madras (1769), Mangalore (1784) and
  Seringapatam (1792).
- The Anglo-Maratha Wars end with the treaties of **Salbai** (1782, first war),
  **Bassein** (1802, which led to the second war) and the defeat of the Peshwa
  in 1818.
- Anglo-Sikh: **Mudki, Ferozeshah, Aliwal** and **Sobraon** (first war, treaty
  of Lahore 1846); **Chilianwala** and **Gujrat** (second war, 1849).

## 4. Wars of Independent India

| War | Year | Notes |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Indo-Pakistani War | 1947 to 48 | Over Jammu and Kashmir; a UN-brokered ceasefire |
| Sino-Indian War | October to November 1962 | Border war with China; ended with a Chinese unilateral ceasefire |
| Indo-Pakistani War | 1965 | Ended with the **Tashkent Declaration** (January 1966) |
| Indo-Pakistani War | 1971 | Pakistani forces in Dhaka surrendered on **16 December 1971**; Bangladesh born |
| Kargil War | 1999 | Indian operation named **Operation Vijay**; Kargil Vijay Diwas on 26 July |

- The **Shimla Agreement** followed the 1971 war in 1972.
- India's Prime Minister during the 1965 war was **Lal Bahadur Shastri**.

## 5. Common Traps

- **Tarain I versus II**: Prithviraj won in 1191 and lost in 1192.
- **Chausa versus Kannauj**: both won by Sher Shah over Humayun, 1539 and 1540.
- **Plassey versus Buxar**: Plassey (1757) broke Bengal's independence; Buxar
  (1764) confirmed British dominance over north India.
- **Haldighati**: the Mughals won on the field; exam options call it a "Mughal
  victory", not a Rajput victory.
- **Panipat I and II** end in Mughal wins, but Panipat III went to the Afghan
  invader.

[example]
Question: "Which battle forced Humayun to flee India?" Chausa (1539) weakened
him, but it was the Battle of Kannauj (Bilgram) in 1540 that sent him into
exile. Both were won by Sher Shah Suri, so the year is the decider: 1539 is
Chausa, 1540 is Kannauj.
[/example]

[summary]
Alexander met Porus at the Hydaspes (326 BCE), and Ashoka won Kalinga in 261
BCE. Tarain I (1191) went to Prithviraj, Tarain II (1192) to Muhammad of Ghor.
The Mughal story runs through Panipat I (1526), Khanwa (1527), Chausa (1539),
Kannauj (1540), Panipat II (1556), Haldighati (1576) and Samugarh (1658), then
Panipat III (1761) to Abdali. The British won at Plassey (1757) and Buxar
(1764), fought four Anglo-Mysore wars ending in 1799, defeated the Marathas
by 1818 and annexed Punjab in 1849. Independent India fought in 1947, 1962,
1965, 1971 and 1999.
[/summary]
